# Definition of Done

A task or PR is **done** only when every box is checked. "It compiles" and
"it looks right" are not done. Evidence beats assertion — paste real output.

## The verification tiers

Verification runs at three tiers. Each has a declared command scope and a
declared claim scope, and **the claim you may make equals the evidence you have**:

| | Tier 1 — inner loop | Tier 2 — task exit | Tier 3 — integration |
|---|---|---|---|
| Trigger | each RED→GREEN step | one task finishes | batch merge; feat exit |
| Tests | the focused test(s) | the full suite, fast mode | the full suite |
| Lint | — | the touched files | the whole tree |
| Typecheck | — | — | yes |
| Build | — | — | yes |
| Coverage | — | — | feat exit only |
| Claim it earns | "this behavior works" | "this task is green" | "the feat is green" |

A Tier-2 run entitles you to say *this task is green*. It never entitles you to
say *the feat is green* — only the Tier-3 gate earns that. Do not run a tier's
checks more than once for the same claim: re-running the suite to confirm a result
you already recorded buys nothing and costs minutes.

Cheap checks stay eager. Lint costs ~0, so it runs at Tier 2 rather than waiting
for integration. Typecheck, build, and coverage are the expensive ones, and they
move to Tier 3.

## Per task (one task from tasks.md)

- [ ] The active spec was read; the change stays within its approved boundary.
- [ ] **Under `tdd`/`tdd-e2e`:** a test failed (RED) for the expected reason before the
      implementation, when the task adds behavior; that test now passes (GREEN) and
      refactoring kept it green.
- [ ] **Under `unit`:** the behavior is covered by a unit test that passes (no RED-first
      evidence required).
- [ ] **Under `tdd-e2e`:** the end-to-end coverage for the affected golden/error flow runs green.
- [ ] The **Tier-2 gate** passed: the full suite (fast mode) plus lint on the touched
      files. Typecheck and build are **not** required at task exit — they belong to Tier 3.
- [ ] The Tier-2 run was made **through the csdd CLI** so the spec carries the evidence (**every flow**):
      `npx @protonspy/csdd spec test-report <feature> --run --lang <lang>` wrote `specs/<feature>/test-report.json`
      (tests green, no open attentions). Missing this evidence is a blocked item.
- [ ] Scope was respected — no opportunistic refactors or unrelated changes.
- [ ] `## Implementation Notes` in tasks.md updated with any cross-task learning.
- [ ] Report lists: task ID, files changed, tests added, commands run, remaining risks.

## Per batch (concurrent tasks merged together)

- [ ] The **Tier-3 gate** ran once for the merged result: full suite, lint, typecheck, build.
- [ ] A red gate produced a fix task naming the failing check, before any further
      dispatch.
- [ ] The record says which tasks composed the batch.

## Per PR (a reviewable slice)

- [ ] Tests cover success, validation, authorization, and external-failure paths for new behavior.
- [ ] The **Tier-3 gate** is green: tests, lint, typecheck, build — run once, at feat exit,
      with coverage collected.
- [ ] `code-reviewer` sub-agent ran and reported no open Blockers.
- [ ] For security-sensitive changes, `security-reviewer` ran and reported no Critical/High.
- [ ] The PR body carries evidence: spec links, tasks completed, the real verification output, and risks.
- [ ] No secrets, tokens, or PII in code, fixtures, logs, or the PR description.

## Evidence block (paste into the PR)

```
## Verification
- npx @protonspy/csdd spec test-report <feature> --run --lang <lang>   ✅  (Tier 3: tests + coverage evidence)
- <lint command>      ✅
- <typecheck command> ✅
- <build command>     ✅
```

If any box cannot be checked, the work is not done — report the blocked item
explicitly rather than claiming completion.
